<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong Chui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">326–396</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Wucheng of Later Yan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Chui was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> who won fame as a general but fled to the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 369 to escape court intrigue. After Fu Jian's defeat at the Fei River in 383 he rebelled, proclaimed himself King of Yan in 384 and emperor in 386, founding the Later Yan with its capital at Zhongshan. He destroyed the Western Yan in 394 and died in 396.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murong-chui.jpg" alt="Murong Chui" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see below · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_49802792_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E7%BA%8C%E7%B7%A8_v.1_(%E6%85%95%E5%AE%B9%E5%9E%82).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
